<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%%%%** Section 1.7
\section{Arrays}
An array is an ordered set of memory elements. For example,
{\tt int j[3]} sets up memory for three integers known as {\tt j[0], j[1], j[2]}.


Graphically, if
\begin{verbatim}
int a[5]=0;  // array of 5 ints
             // each int is 2 bytes
\end{verbatim}

\begin{tabular}{l|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
Element name & {\tt a[0]} & {\tt a[1]} & {\tt a[2]} & {\tt a[3]} & {\tt a[4]} \\ \hline
Address      & 3000 & 3002 & 3004 & 3006 & 3008 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}

%%%%** Section 1.8
\subsection{Initializing Arrays}


When you declare an array such as
\begin{verbatim}
int a[5];
\end{verbatim}
Enough storage is allocated for 5 integers (typically 5$\times$32 bits
or 40 bytes). But you should not count on any initial value.

To initialize the array use one of two methods:
\begin{verbatim}
int q[3] = {0,1,2};

or

int i, q[3];

...

for (i=0;i<3;i++) q[i] = 0;
\end{verbatim}


%%%%** Section 1.9
\subsection{Arrays and Pointers}
Arrays are implemented inside {\tt C} just like pointers.

Example:
\begin{verbatim}
int a[10], *p;
p = &a[0];
a[3]  <----->  *(p+3)
are exactly the same.

AND

a <------->  &a[0]
are exactly the same.
\end{verbatim}
<*chn>
You can use the array name ({\tt a}) without the subscript
({\tt [1]}) and it is identical to a pointer to the first element.
Also, any pointer can be
subscripted whether it was declared as an array or not.

<*>
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsection*{Lewis Approach}

\begin{verbatim}
#define DISP_BUFFER 0xB8000
int row, col;
char disp_char;
char *p;
//       Let's display "A" in row 4, col 20
disp_char = 'A';
row = 4;        // for example ...
col = 20;       //  "    "

p = (char *) (DISP_BUFFER + 2 *(80*row + col))
*p = disp_char;
\end{verbatim}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.6
\subsection{Exercise in Class}
 Complete this example: write code to
 set the byte which controls the {\bf color}
 of the character {\tt A} we just displayed.

 Assume the color byte should be
 assigned the value {\tt COLOR\_BYTE}.

<*chn> The color byte has 2 4-bit fields which
set the background color (16 possible colors)
and the character color (16 possible colors).
<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<ch>\vspace{2.0in}
<*ns>
\subsection*{Answer}
\begin{verbatim}

#define COLOR_BYTE  0xBB  // (made up hex value)

int row, col;
char *p;
row = 4;
col = 20;
p = (char *) ((DISP_BUFFER + 2 *(80*row + col))  + 1 )
*p = COLOR_BYTE;
\end{verbatim}

<*>
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.10
\subsection{Array/Pointer Example}

To find the character at each row/col, we had \\
{\tt
p = (char *) (DISP\_BUFFER + 2 *(80*row + col)} \\
Can we express this as an array reference?

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\subsubsection*{Yes}

{\tt
p = (char *) (DISP\_BUFFER + 2 *(80*row + col)); \\
*p = 'A';} \\
is just the same as
\begin{verbatim}
char *disp_buff = DISP_BUFFER;

disp_buff[2 *(80*row + col)] = 'A';
\end{verbatim}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Even more succinctly:
\begin{verbatim}
typedef char      CELL[2];  // a CELL is 2 chars
typedef CELL      ROW[80];  // a ROW is 80 CELLs

ROW *disp_buff = (ROW *)DISP_BUFFER;

disp_buff[row][col][0] = 'A';
disp_buff[row][col][1] = COLOR_BYTE ;
\end{verbatim}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.11
\subsection{Exercise in Class}


Modify the scheme in Lewis, sec 3.6, so that we
can address display characters and display colors
each in a separate row/column array.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
<ch>\vspace{2.5in}
<*sn>
\subsubsection*{Answer}

\begin{verbatim}

typedef char CELL[2]    // a CELL is 2 chars

CELL *graphics_chars =  (CELL *)DISP_BUFFER;
CELL *graphics_colors = (CELL *)(DISP_BUFFER+1);
  // NOT: graphics_colors = 1 + graphics_chars -- Why?

graphics_chars[80*row + col] = 'A';
graphics_colors[80*row + col] = COLOR_BYTE;

\end{verbatim}

We could have used {\tt [0]} after each array reference but we
didn't have to --- why not?

<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 2 
\section{Generic ({\tt void}) Pointers}

Sometimes we want a pointer which is not locked to a
specific type.  It can potentially point to anything.

\begin{verbatim}
void *name ;   // declare a generic pointer called name
\end{verbatim}

\begin{itemize}
        \item {\tt name} can point to anything in the computer.
        \item {\tt name} cannot be dereferenced with *
        \item Must instead assign value of {\tt void}
        pointer  to a pointer of the type you want. \\
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsubsection*{Examples}
\begin{verbatim}
void* myGenericPtr;
int t, *ip, myvalue = 3;
myGenericPtr = &myvalue;  // OK
t = *myGenericPtr;  // NO!!

//*****************

ip = nyGenericPtr;
t = *ip;   // OK!!
\end{verbatim}



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 2.1
\subsection{Null Pointer}

If a pointer has the value {\tt NULL}, it points
to nothing.   {\tt NULL } is a predefined constant
in {\tt <stddef.h>} or use\\
{\tt \#define NULL 0}

{\tt NULL} is illegal to dereference.

{\tt NULL } can be tested for:

\begin{verbatim}
int i,*ip = NULL;

[...]

if(ip == NULL) {
    // I haven't defined ip yet
    }
    else {  // OK, now I can use it!
    i = *ip;}
\end{verbatim}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 2.2
\subsection{Function Pointers}

C can have pointers to functions.

\begin{verbatim}
type (* functionpointer)(arg list)
\end{verbatim}
Examples
\begin{verbatim}
int(*IntFuncPtr)();
\\   IntFuncPtr is a pointer to a function with
\\    no arguments which returns an int

double(*doubleFuncPtr)(int arg1, char arg2)
\\   doubleFuncPtr is a pointer to a function with
\\     an int and a char arg which returns a double.
\end{verbatim}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
Assignment to function pointers:

\begin{verbatim}
int (* IFP)(int x) = NULL;    // empty function pointer
int realfunction( int x);     // an actual function

IFP = &realfunction;
IFP = realfunction;    // can skip &
\end{verbatim}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
Dereferencing function pointers:

\begin{verbatim}

(*IFP)(5)    // call function realfunction with arg 5

IFP(5)       // can also use function pointer just like
             // original function name
\end{verbatim}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3 
\section{Pointer Arithmetic}

A powerful feature of pointers is the ability to compute with them like
integers.  However only some operations are allowed with pointers.

Allowed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Add a scalar to a pointer
  \item Subtract pointers
\end{itemize}

Not Allowed:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Add two pointers
  \item Multiply or Divide Pointers
  \item Multiply by a scalar
  \item Divide by a scalar
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Example: Find the midpoint in an array.}

\begin{verbatim}
#define SIZE 100
int  length, buffer[SIZE];
int *ptr1, *ptr2, *ptr3;

ptr1 = buffer;          // points to start of array
ptr2 = ptr1 + 100;      // points to end of array
length = ptr2 - ptr1;   // length = 100
ptr3 = ptr1 + length/2  // ptr3 points to mid-point of array

\end{verbatim}

%%%%** Section 3.1
\subsection{Pointer comparisons}

{\tt ==, !=} \hspace{0.5in} Determine if two pointers are equal or not.

{\tt <, <=, >=, >} \hspace{0.5in} Which pointer points to a higher
address in memory?  Which way will subtraction come out?





